.TH XFMT 1 LOCAL.SH NAMExfmt \- a simple text formatter.SH SYNOPSIS.B xfmt[.B \-i] [.B \-j] [.B \-m] [.B \-x] [.B \-o] [.B \-u] [.B \-c] [.B \-l n] [.B \-p n] [.B \-t n] [ filenames ].SH DESCRIPTION.I Xfmtis a simple text formatter.  With no options present, the defaultbehavior of.I xfmtis to copy its input (from specified files or from stdin, if no filesare specified) to its output but with input reformatted into lines nolonger than 72 characters long.  A blank line is taken to indicate thebeginning of a new paragraph.  Lines that begin with a period arepreserved..PPThe following options modify this behavior:.TP.B \-iThis option will cause.I xfmt to preserve indentations.  Blanks or tabs at the beginning of a linewill be considered the beginning of a new paragraph..TP.B \-jJustify output lines.  (The default behavior of.I xfmtis to output lines with a ragged right margin)..TP.B \-mInterpret a few commonly used.I nroff-man commands.Those understood (in some fashion) are:.DS .B .I .R .S .G .SM .C .IR .BR .RI .BI .RB .IB .SH .SS .TH .PP .P .LP .TP .IP .HP .RS .RE .DS .DE .NT .NE .VS .VE .CT .PN .MS .UC (ignored) .DT (ignored) .PD (ignored).DE.spAnd a few other nroff commands that occur commonly in manpages are recognized:.DS \\fI \\fB \\fR \\fP \\fC .sp .br .nf .fi .ti .\\" \\*(lq \\*(rq .ta (ignored).DE.spMore details can be found in the document.IR cmds.tex ..TP.B \-xInterpret a few commonly used.I TeXcommands.Those understood (in some fashion) are:.DS \\vskip \\bigskip \\medskip \\smallskip \\$ \\# \\{ \\} \\& \\% \\^ \\_ \\~ \\' \\" \\\\ \\it \\sl \\bf \\rm \\tt \\TeX \\centerline \\rightline \\raggedright \\obeylines \\item { } \\(space) ~ $ (ignored) \\backslash \\beginsection.DE.TP.B \-oProduce bold and underlined text with overstriking.If the terminal and termcap entries permit, the bold and underlinedportions can be seen on the screen by feeding.I xfmtoutput to.MS ul 1 .Bold is produced by fonts B/bf, S/tt, and underlinedby I/it, G/sl..TP.B \-uProduce bold, underlined, reversed, and blinking text for terminaldisplay (using termcap md, us, mr, and mb entries, respectively).This is useful only if one of the options -m or -x is also chosen.Bold is produced by font B/bf, underlined by I/it, reversed by S/tt,and blinking by G/sl..TP.B \-cFor C code, display comments reversed, flow-of-control keywordsin bold, storage-class keywords underlined, and pre-processorkeywords blinking.No formatting is done.  The -u option is assumed unless the -ooption is given..TP.B \-l nThis option can be used to change the line length from the default of 72characters..TP.B \-p nChange the page offset.  This option will cause the output lines to beoffset the indicated number of columns from the left..TP.B \-t nSet tab size.  This option is useful only with the.B \-i or.B \-coptions, or for text in the scope of .nf, .EX, .DS, \obeylines or \obeyspaces,since otherwise tabs are treated the same as space characters..SH EXAMPLESDisplay a man page.I binmail.1on the screen with right justification, a page at a time:.EXxfmt -jmu binmail.1 | more -f.EEDisplay a man page with underlining, but no bold face:.EXxfmt -mo binmail.1 | more -f.EEPrepare a preformatted man page to be displayed with.MS ul 1 :.EXxfmt -mo binmail.1 >binmail.doc.EEDisplay high-lighted C source code on the screen:.EXxfmt -c \fIfile\fR.c.EE.SH TIPSIn.IR vi ,you can define a macro that will reformat paragraphs by typing.I ":map V {!}xfmt^M" or by putting the line.RS.I "map V {!}xfmt^M".REin your.I .exrcfile.  After defining this macro, pressing.I V will cause the paragraph under the cursor to be reformatted.  (Use the.I ukey to.I undoif necessary..SH "SEE ALSO"nroff(1), vi(1), ul(1).SH BUGSTeX command interpretation doesn't have a chance of working exceptfor files prepared with the limitations of xfmt in mind.Nroff -man interpretation has maybe a 50-50 chance..SH AUTHORSoriginal version: bgray@marque.mu.edu (Bill Gray).brTeX, man, C mods: lee@uhccux.uhcc.hawaii.edu (Greg Lee)